Context Propagation in Distributed Tracing
Lab Objectives
By the end of this lab, students will be able to:

Understand the importance of trace context propagation in distributed systems
Implement the W3C Trace Context standard for maintaining trace continuity across services
Configure OpenTelemetry for automatic context propagation with Flask and requests
Track a single distributed trace across three cooperating microservices
Analyze trace data in Jaeger to verify context propagation and identify latency in the request chain
Prerequisites
Before starting this lab, students should have:

Basic understanding of microservices architecture
Familiarity with HTTP requests and responses
Knowledge of Python programming fundamentals
Experience with command-line operations in Linux
Basic knowledge of containerization concepts (Docker)
This lab uses a Linux machine with Python 3.8+ and Docker available. Install the required tools with the following commands:

sudo apt update
sudo apt install -y python3 python3-pip python3-venv curl docker.io docker-compose
sudo systemctl start docker
sudo systemctl enable docker
sudo usermod -aG docker $USER
Log out and back in for the Docker group change to take effect, then verify:

docker --version
docker-compose --version
Create a working directory and Python virtual environment:

mkdir -p ~/tracing-lab
cd ~/tracing-lab
python3 -m venv venv
source venv/bin/activate
pip install --upgrade pip
pip install flask==3.0.3 requests==2.32.3 \
  opentelemetry-api==1.27.0 \
  opentelemetry-sdk==1.27.0 \
  opentelemetry-instrumentation-flask==0.48b0 \
  opentelemetry-instrumentation-requests==0.48b0 \
  opentelemetry-exporter-jaeger==1.21.0
Note: opentelemetry-exporter-jaeger is deprecated upstream in favor of OTLP, but it remains fully functional and is used here because it requires no collector configuration beyond the Jaeger all-in-one image, keeping the lab focused on context propagation rather than exporter setup.

Remember to run source ~/tracing-lab/venv/bin/activate in every new terminal session before running any Python command in this lab.

