Subtask 3.3: Verify Trace Continuity in Jaeger
Query the Jaeger API directly to confirm the trace spans multiple services:

curl -s "http://localhost:16686/api/services" | python3 -m json.tool
Deliverable / expected result: the data array now lists "user-service", "order-service", and "inventory-service", confirming all three services reported spans to the same backend.

Fetch recent traces for the user-service and inspect the span count:

curl -s "http://localhost:16686/api/traces?service=user-service&limit=5" | python3 -m json.tool | head -60
In the JSON output, find a single trace object under data and confirm its spans array includes entries whose process.serviceName values are user-service, order-service, and inventory-service, and that every span shares the same traceID. This is the definitive proof of context propagation: a single traceID value connects independently generated spans across three separate Python processes.

For visual verification, open the Jaeger UI (use your machine's external IP if accessing remotely, port 16686):

echo "Jaeger UI: http://localhost:16686"
In the UI: select user-service from the Service dropdown, click Find Traces, open the most recent trace, and confirm the timeline view shows nested spans from user-service, order-service, and inventory-service under a single trace, with process_individual_order and database_lookup spans nested beneath the service-level spans.

Deliverable for Task 3: at least one trace visible in the Jaeger UI (or via the /api/traces endpoint) whose spans include all three services under a single traceID, along with the JSON output from Subtask 3.2 showing the fully populated nested response.

Expected Outcomes
Three independently running Flask services that automatically propagate W3C Trace Context (traceparent header) across HTTP calls using OpenTelemetry's inject/extract functions
A verified end-to-end trace in Jaeger showing a single traceID spanning user-service, order-service, and inventory-service, with correctly nested parent-child spans
Practical experience reading span attributes (user.id, order.id, inventory.available) and using the Jaeger UI and query API to confirm propagation succeeded
Troubleshooting
Symptom: traces show up per-service but never combine into a single multi-service trace in Jaeger. Diagnostic hint: check that inject(headers) is called before the outbound requests.get() call and that the receiving service calls extract(request.headers) and passes the resulting context into start_as_current_span. A missing inject or extract call breaks the parent-child link even though each service still reports spans independently.
Symptom: curl http://localhost:5001/user/<id> hangs or returns a connection error referencing port 5002 or 5003. Diagnostic hint: confirm all three Flask processes are actually running (ps aux | grep app.py) and that no other process is already bound to ports 5001-5003 (ss -tlnp | grep -E '5001|5002|5003'); a downstream service that failed to start silently breaks the chain even though the entry-point service still responds with an empty orders list.
Conclusion
In this lab, students built a three-service distributed system in Flask and instrumented it with OpenTelemetry to propagate W3C Trace Context across HTTP boundaries using the inject and extract propagation API. By manually injecting the traceparent header on outbound calls and extracting it on inbound requests, each service was able to contribute spans to a single shared trace, which was verified both through direct inspection of the Jaeger API's traceID field and visually in the Jaeger UI's trace timeline. Students should now understand why context propagation is the foundation of distributed tracing: without it, each service would only ever produce isolated, disconnected spans, making it impossible to reconstruct the true path and latency profile of a request as it moves through a microservice architecture. As a next step, students can extend this lab by adding baggage propagation for cross-cutting metadata such as user tier or request source, or by introducing deliberate latency and error conditions in the inventory service to practice diagnosing bottlenecks using the same trace data.
